  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nWe propose a two-phase optimization strategy: initial candidate generation followed by gradient-based refinement. This approach balances exploration through diverse starting points and exploitation via gradient descent, ensuring feasibility throughout.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n- The decision variable is h \u2208 [0,1]^{n_points}, with \u2211h = n_points/2.\n- The objective is to minimize C\u2085 = max_k (h \u2217 (1-h))[k] * (2 / n_points).\n- Each optimization step ensures 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and \u2211h = n_points/2.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n\nA. Initialization\n1. Use n_points=150.\n2. Start with 20 initial candidates from initial_h_values or a structured initialization.\n\nB. Candidate Generation\n1. Perturb each candidate with Gaussian noise and systematic shifts.\n\nC. Local Refinement\n1. For each candidate, compute gradient and update h using gradient descent.\n2. Project to feasibility after each step.\n\nD. Diversity and Termination\n1. Add new candidates periodically.\n2. Track best and terminate on stagnation.",
